Markdown renderer — Markdown Render Speed 02

This repository contains an independently structured, modified implementation
of a Markdown renderer informed by marked 18.1.3. The marked project was
created by Christopher Jeffrey and is maintained by the marked contributors.
Project page: https://github.com/markedjs/marked

Copyright (c) 2011–2024 the marked contributors. Marked is distributed under
the MIT License. This fork preserves that attribution and is separately
licensed under the MIT terms in LICENSE.

The parser and driver in this repository contain original, independently
written modifications by vora95 and contributors, including run-aware inline
code and delimiter handling, link destination parsing, block and list
recognition, fenced code collection, and an independent batch driver. These
files are not a copy of the upstream distribution. The upstream copyright and
MIT attribution above are retained for the marked-derived work.

Additional independently authored refinements include multiline quoted HTML
tag scanning and balanced-parenthesis handling for bare GFM URLs.
Further independent work in this fork covers list continuation and tightness,
CommonMark reference links, delimiter/link interactions, extended GFM
autolinks, and raw HTML attribute validation.
Reference definitions are collected during block parsing in a separate
collection pass so code and HTML blocks do not contribute definitions; this
block-aware behavior is independently authored in this fork.
The current emphasis pass independently collects delimiter runs, applies
flanking and run-of-three checks, and emits paired tags while preserving unused
markers. This implementation remains a modified fork and does not include the
upstream parser distribution.

This revision also adjusts HTML block separators, table body rows (including
empty cells and rows without pipe separators), list looseness, fenced block
boundaries, blockquote recognition, and autolink URL encoding in independently
authored parser code.

The repository also carries independent compatibility changes for the practice
document set, including list-item fence boundary handling and run-aware
emphasis pairing; these changes retain the upstream attribution above.

This revision also includes independently authored reference-definition validation that rejects an attached title without separator whitespace.

This revision further refines list continuation so block starts are checked
against the item's content indentation while plain paragraph text may continue
lazily.

The 2026 revision also includes an independently authored nested-container
continuation refinement for list and blockquote boundaries.
